  "id": "soviet_mass_song",
  "name": "Massovaya pesnya (Soviet mass song and Red Army choir)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "The Soviet mass song (massovaya pesnya), a strophic song with a singable refrain written for collective singing at work, on the march and at public celebrations, was promoted from the 1930s by composers such as Isaak Dunayevsky, Alexander Alexandrov and Matvey Blanter and spread by radio, film and records. Its emblematic performer is the Alexandrov Red Army Song and Dance Ensemble, founded in 1928, whose male choir and soloists sing with an orchestra that joins folk instruments such as bayan, balalaika and domra to brass and winds.",
  "recipe": "classic Eastern European mid-1930s massovaya pesnya, functional Song, europop branch, Slavic bard ballad poetry, European protest song arbeiterlied and kampflied kuban cossack song. choir ensemble, b system buttons stradella bass Russian tradition estrada stage Soviet era bayan russian, prima Soviet orchestra canon strummed balalaika, dance rhythm strummed domra, gold brass 85 15 warmer trumpet. moscow state label 1964-present major-flagship moscow melodiya studio. Neumann U47, German V72 tube pre, German tube console, Telefunken u73b broadcast comp, broadcast loudness EQ. tape 15ips hall reverb.",
